::ALG1_U01_PT_Q01::
Question ID:<br>ALG1_U01_PT_Q01<br><br>MLA Standard:<br>MLA.A1.FND.4<br><br>Question:<br>In the expression 7x + 4, what is the coefficient? {
~A. x#This is the variable, not the coefficient. The coefficient is the numerical factor.
=B. 7#Correct. The coefficient is the number multiplying the variable x.
~C. 4#This is the constant term. It does not multiply the variable.
~D. 11#This is the sum of 7 and 4, not a named part of the expression.
}

::ALG1_U01_PT_Q02::
Question ID:<br>ALG1_U01_PT_Q02<br><br>MLA Standard:<br>MLA.A1.FND.4<br><br>Question:<br>Evaluate 2x + 3 when x = 5. {
~A. 10#This evaluates 2x but leaves out the +3. Substitute first, then complete the whole expression.
~B. 25#This multiplies 2 and 5, then attaches the 3 incorrectly. Follow order of operations.
~C. 53#This places 5 and 3 next to each other instead of adding after multiplication.
=D. 13#Correct. Substitute 5 for x: 2(5) + 3 = 10 + 3 = 13.
}

::ALG1_U01_PT_Q03::
Question ID:<br>ALG1_U01_PT_Q03<br><br>MLA Standard:<br>MLA.A1.FND.4<br><br>Question:<br>Evaluate -6 + 14. {
=A. 8#Correct. Moving 14 units right from -6 lands at 8.
~B. -20#This adds the absolute values and keeps the negative sign. The positive value has greater absolute value.
~C. -8#This has the sign reversed. The result is positive because 14 is farther from zero than -6.
~D. 20#This adds the absolute values but ignores the sign relationship.
}

::ALG1_U01_PT_Q04::
Question ID:<br>ALG1_U01_PT_Q04<br><br>MLA Standard:<br>MLA.A1.FND.4<br><br>Question:<br>Which value is equivalent to 0.4? {
~A. 4%#This moves the decimal one place too few. 0.4 is 40%.
~B. 400%#This moves the decimal one place too many.
=C. 40%#Correct. 0.4 multiplied by 100 equals 40%.
~D. 0.04%#This makes the value much smaller than 0.4.
}

::ALG1_U01_PT_Q05::
Question ID:<br>ALG1_U01_PT_Q05<br><br>MLA Standard:<br>MLA.A1.FND.1<br><br>Question:<br>Evaluate 3<sup>2</sup>. {
~A. 6#This multiplies 3 by 2. An exponent means repeated multiplication.
=B. 9#Correct. 3<sup>2</sup> = 3 &middot; 3 = 9.
~C. 5#This adds the base and exponent. Exponents do not mean addition.
~D. 8#This uses 2<sup>3</sup> instead of 3<sup>2</sup>.
}

::ALG1_U01_PT_Q06::
Question ID:<br>ALG1_U01_PT_Q06<br><br>MLA Standard:<br>MLA.A1.FND.1<br><br>Question:<br>Evaluate 16<sup><sup>1</sup>&frasl;<sub>2</sub></sup>. {
=A. 4#Correct. 16<sup><sup>1</sup>&frasl;<sub>2</sub></sup> is the square root of 16, which is 4.
~B. 8#This divides 16 by 2. An exponent of <sup>1</sup>&frasl;<sub>2</sub> means square root.
~C. 32#This multiplies 16 by 2 instead of finding the square root.
~D. 256#This squares 16 instead of finding the square root.
}

::ALG1_U01_PT_Q07::
Question ID:<br>ALG1_U01_PT_Q07<br><br>MLA Standard:<br>MLA.A1.FND.1<br><br>Question:<br>Evaluate 2<sup>3</sup> &middot; 2<sup>2</sup>. {
~A. 12#This multiplies visible numbers incorrectly. Use exponent rules or repeated multiplication.
~B. 64#This multiplies exponents to get too large a power. Add exponents when multiplying powers with the same base.
~C. 10#This adds 8 and 2 or combines values incorrectly.
=D. 32#Correct. 2<sup>3</sup> &middot; 2<sup>2</sup> = 2<sup>5</sup> = 32.
}

::ALG1_U01_PT_Q08::
Question ID:<br>ALG1_U01_PT_Q08<br><br>MLA Standard:<br>MLA.A1.FND.2<br><br>Question:<br>Rewrite x<sup>4</sup> &middot; x<sup>3</sup>. {
~A. x<sup>12</sup>#This multiplies the exponents. For product of powers with the same base, add the exponents.
~B. 2x<sup>7</sup>#This adds an extra coefficient. There is no coefficient 2 in the expression.
=C. x<sup>7</sup>#Correct. x<sup>4</sup> &middot; x<sup>3</sup> = x<sup>4+3</sup> = x<sup>7</sup>.
~D. x#This subtracts exponents instead of adding them.
}

::ALG1_U01_PT_Q09::
Question ID:<br>ALG1_U01_PT_Q09<br><br>MLA Standard:<br>MLA.A1.FND.2<br><br>Question:<br>Simplify 5a + 3a. {
~A. 15a#This multiplies the coefficients. Like terms are combined by adding coefficients.
=B. 8a#Correct. 5a and 3a are like terms, so 5a + 3a = 8a.
~C. 8a<sup>2</sup>#This changes the variable part. Combining like terms keeps the variable part the same.
~D. 2a#This subtracts the coefficients instead of adding them.
}

::ALG1_U01_PT_Q10::
Question ID:<br>ALG1_U01_PT_Q10<br><br>MLA Standard:<br>MLA.A1.FND.2<br><br>Question:<br>Rewrite 3(x + 4) using the distributive property. {
~A. 3x + 4#This multiplies 3 by x but not by 4. Distribute to every term inside the parentheses.
~B. 7x#This adds 3 and 4 and attaches x. The distributive property uses multiplication.
~C. 12x#This multiplies 3 and 4 but does not include the x-term correctly.
=D. 3x + 12#Correct. Multiply 3 by x and multiply 3 by 4.
}
